      <h2>plex-db-analyzer: a data platform on a homelab</h2>
      <p>A personal side project, built and run on the <a href='/server.html'>homelab</a> cluster. The code is private, so this page covers how it is put together rather than how to run it.</p>

      <h2 id='what'>What it is <small>2026</small></h2>
      <p>plex-db-analyzer ingests Plex Media Server database backups (the <code>.zip</code> the server writes) and charts what is in them: codecs, storage, watch history, bandwidth and library growth, all on a D3 dashboard.</p>

      <h2 id='services'>From monolith to services</h2>
      <p>It started life as a monolith: Node.js, Express and SQLite in one process. It was rewritten as eight Go services and a static dashboard, each doing one job:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>ingest</strong> - accepts the uploaded backup, unpacks it and publishes the slices the other services need</li>
        <li><strong>content</strong>, <strong>storage</strong>, <strong>codecs</strong>, <strong>watch</strong>, <strong>bandwidth</strong> and <strong>people</strong> - the domain services, each answering questions about one part of the data</li>
        <li><strong>overview-aggregator</strong> - pulls the domain APIs together into a single summary</li>
        <li><strong>dashboard</strong> - the D3 front end, served by nginx</li>
      </ul>
      <p>They run on Knative Serving, and Knative Eventing fans each uploaded backup out to the domain services through a dedicated broker and per-service triggers. The design follows a strict API-first rule: each domain service owns its own database and its own API, no service reads another's tables, and there are no internal-only interfaces. Every API is built to be exposed as-is.</p>

